---
import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
import ModellingVisual from '@/components/modelling/ModellingVisual.astro';
const provenance = `@prefix ex: <https://example.org/harbour-court/> .
@prefix model: <https://example.org/property-model/> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

ex:inspection a model:Inspection, prov:Activity ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-12T09:00:00+01:00"^^xsd:dateTime .
ex:report-v1 a model:ReportVersion, prov:Entity ;
    model:reportsInspection ex:inspection ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-08-14T10:00:00+01:00"^^xsd:dateTime .
ex:report-v2 a model:ReportVersion, prov:Entity ;
    model:reportsInspection ex:inspection ;
    prov:wasRevisionOf ex:report-v1 ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-08-20T11:00:00+01:00"^^xsd:dateTime .`;
---

<ModellingLayout title="Evidence and time: preserve what happened and what was recorded" description="An inspection, a claim about its subject and a report describing it have different identities and timelines. Provenance explains a statement’s production and sources; it does not turn a statement into a verified fact." claim="method">
  <section aria-labelledby="dossier">
    <h2 id="dossier">Follow one inspection through two reports</h2>
    <p>In the fictional Harbour Court dossier, the inspection takes place on 12 August 2026. Report version 1 is produced on 14 August. A correction produces report version 2 on 20 August. Both report versions concern the same inspection in this illustration. A consumer must be able to discover that difference rather than treating the latest document as evidence of a later visit.</p>
    <p>Distinguish the physical subject, the inspection activity, the statement about its findings and the report version carrying that statement. The building contains two dwellings, so identifying only the address may be insufficient to determine the inspection’s subject. A correction to the subject reference is itself important evidence; it must not silently alter the identity of the building or erase the earlier record.</p>
    <p>The dates and resources are invented for explanation. They do not certify a real inspection, an approved OPDA provenance model or current candidate support for every pattern described here.</p>
  </section>

<figure class="modelling-figure" data-modelling-figure="evidence-timeline">
    <ModellingVisual kind="evidence-timeline" />
    <ol class="modelling-steps">
      <li><time datetime="2026-08-12">12 August 2026</time>: inspection activity occurs.</li>
      <li><time datetime="2026-08-14">14 August 2026</time>: report version 1 is generated.</li>
      <li><time datetime="2026-08-20">20 August 2026</time>: corrected report version 2 is generated, referring to the same inspection.</li>
    </ol>
    <figcaption>A new document version is not a new inspection. The time a receiving system records either report is a further, separately described fact.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section aria-labelledby="provenance">
    <h2 id="provenance">Describe entities, activities and responsible agents</h2>
    <p>PROV-O provides distinctions between an entity, an activity that uses or produces entities, and an agent associated with responsibility. A report version can be an entity; producing or revising it can be an activity. Attribution to an agent and derivation from a source answer different questions and should not be interchanged merely because both look like “where this came from”. <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/#description-starting-point-terms">PROV-O §§3.1 and 4.1</a> introduces those terms.</p>
    <p>The provenance of an ontology term is another use of this discipline. Which source definition supported the modelling choice? Which extraction or editorial activity produced the candidate? Which version of the source was used? That is not the same as provenance of an inspection finding in a property transaction. Both may be needed, but they describe different entities and activities.</p>
    <p>A source-file path is a literal location string, not automatically a PROV Location resource. The OPDA method’s 28 August amendment makes its source-file datatype property independent of <code>prov:atLocation</code>; it must not be a datatype subproperty of that object property. Retaining useful lineage does not justify violating the reused vocabulary’s property kinds.</p>
  </section>

  <details>
    <summary>Inspect report revision without inventing another inspection</summary>
    <p>Times below are illustrative additions to the teaching dates. The excerpt does not show complete activities, attribution, claim qualification or vocabulary definitions.</p>
    <pre><code>{provenance}</code></pre>
    <p>The two report links identify the same inspection resource. Revision relates the report entities. It does not assert that a later document changed the past event or that every claim in either version is true.</p>
  </details>

  <section aria-labelledby="granularity">
    <h2 id="granularity">Choose provenance granularity by the claim</h2>
    <p>Per-resource provenance is economical when all relevant statements about an immutable generated resource share the same source and attribution. It becomes misleading if one statement is later added from a different source while the resource still appears to have uniform provenance.</p>
    <p>The selected method uses resource-level provenance by default, with statement-level identification when attribution or confidence differs between fields. An immutable-unit assumption is a precondition to the simpler pattern, not a fact to assume about all current outputs. When that precondition no longer holds, change the provenance representation rather than hiding the divergence.</p>
    <p>For Harbour Court, the inspection’s subject, date and conclusion might come from different portions or versions of evidence. A single “verified” badge cannot express that. Identify the particular claim and the evidence supporting it, and retain uncertainty where the source cannot establish the answer. Reification makes a statement addressable; it does not establish its truth.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="two-times">
    <h2 id="two-times">Separate valid time from recorded time</h2>
    <p>Valid time says when a state or claim applies in the modelled world. Recorded or transaction time says when the system acquired or recorded the information. A correction entered on 20 August may concern an observation on 12 August. Collapsing those times makes it impossible to ask what was believed at a particular time and what period that belief concerned.</p>
    <p>The adopted temporal pattern uses an immutable transition-record entity for a change of state. Its effective time points to an OWL-Time instant. Its generation time, when supplied, describes the record; the recording activity is linked separately. Activities use activity start/end properties, not a generation timestamp borrowed from an entity pattern.</p>
    <p>OWL-Time supplies instants, intervals and temporal relationships. A local state-transition relation supplies the domain-specific meaning of changing state; the time vocabulary alone does not define a lifecycle. The adopted profile uses a deliberately limited temporal-relation selection rather than claiming every possible temporal inference is operational. <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/owl-time/#time:Instant">OWL-Time §4.1</a> defines its temporal classes.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="state">
    <h2 id="state">Record transitions; derive snapshots when useful</h2>
    <p>The selected pattern treats the transition chain as primary and a point-in-time snapshot as an optional derived view. A transition identifies the entity, its previous and new state, and its effective time. State values belong to a governed closed state space. An explicit allowed-successor relation describes one-step adjacency; SKOS hierarchy must not be used as a substitute.</p>
    <p>This distinction matters when asking “what was the transaction’s status on 15 August?”. A stored “current status” value cannot by itself answer a historical question. Conversely, adopting a temporal concern does not require every candidate to materialise a full snapshot history before it has a use case.</p>
    <p>Do not confuse state history with identity. Subdivision of Flat 1 remains an unresolved identity case in this example. Recording a state transition would assume the bearer persists, which is precisely what the reviewers have not yet established. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/roles-and-phases">roles and phases</a> chapter makes that boundary explicit.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="quality">
    <h2 id="quality">Evidence supports assessment; it is not a quality guarantee</h2>
    <p>Confidence in an extraction is not confidence in the source’s factual claim. A tool might reproduce a report perfectly while the report itself is incomplete or mistaken. The OPDA method’s confidence annotation has no automatically operationalised ISO 25012 or DQV quality meaning. Do not imply a quality certification simply because those standards appear in a category’s references.</p>
    <p>The failure case is a page showing “latest inspection: 20 August” because it selected the newest report timestamp. The repair is to preserve the event/report distinction and query the time appropriate to the question. A validation result can check the presence and datatype of a date; human and source evidence establish what that date means.</p>
    <p>Temporal patterns are also scoped. The active strategic relationships in <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/context-map-records">context maps</a> explicitly do not use the report revision/history model. One reusable vocabulary does not make every resource share one lifecycle.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="source-contract">
    <h2 id="source-contract">Source contract</h2>
    <p>Required method: OPDA <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0052">ODR-0052</a> R2–R4 and its 28 August source-file correction; <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0053">ODR-0053</a> R2–R5 and its 28 August transition-record/time correction; <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0044">ODR-0044</a>’s asserted/derived distinction. <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0056">ODR-0056</a> reserves mapping confidence for its SSSOM profile. <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0064">ODR-0064</a>’s later active-map-only exception remains separate.</p>
    <p>Adoption provenance: upstream revision <code>67174057e6384b79d0b28b7736fe70a66e112895</code>, 5 September 2026. See <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/standards-and-decisions">standards and decisions</a> for authority and scope. Required provenance patterns, fictional report data and demonstrated current-candidate coverage are not interchangeable.</p>
  </section>
</ModellingLayout>
